                                  UTM HELP
===============================================================================
To print this help file: in DOS, type "PRINT UTM.HLP"
===============================================================================

   A U(niversal) T(uring) M(achine) is a Turing state table which enables TM*
theoretically to run any other Turing machine described on the Turing input
tape (the simulated or "pseudo-TM").  The UTM here is modeled along the lines
of:

                Marvin Minsky
                COMPUTATION: FINITE AND INFINITE MACHINES
                [Chapters 6-7]
                Prentice-Hall, Englewood Cliffs, NJ (1967)


                               SPECIAL SYMBOLS
                               ---------------
To enable this specific UTM machine to operate best in the TM* environment,
special provisions have been added:
   The UTM tape contains only the symbols: %!0, %!1, %!A, %!B, %!M, %!S, %!X,
   %!Y.
   The numerals %!0 and %!1 are standard TM state table format, but the letters
   have special functions.

   %!A is used by the UTM to mark temporarily the location of a %!0 on its
     tape;
   %!B is likewise used to mark temporarily a %!1.
   %!Mark indicates the location of the pseudo-TM read/write head on the tape
     (as the UTM sees it).
   %!Stop shows when the pseudo-tape operations have been completed.
   %!X is used to mark boundaries between pseudo-TM quintuples on the UTM tape,
     and
   %!Y is used to mark the beginning and end of the machine condition plus the
     state table area.

   %!? in the UTM state-table is used as a 'wild-card' ELSE clause. That is, if
     there is no applicable input for that state, it will execute the output,
     move, etc., from the %!? entry.  It is only used in the UTM table and
     should not appear on the tape.


                               UTM Input Tape
                               --------------
The UTM tape has a special format:

 ...000M11101110Y000X0000001X0010101X0101010X0110111Y000...
                |   ^
             ---    |---
  binary    |  machine  |  canonical description of pseudo-TM
pseudo-tape | condition |  state-table quintuples

[This example description of a pseudo-TM is an adding machine that takes as
 input two numbers in unary notation (separated by one zero) on its input tape.
 Its inital tape is ...000^011101110. ]
                           ^
There must be exactly two Y's in the entire description of the pseudo-TM.  The
leftmost Y marks the end of the pseudo-tape and the begining of the machine
condition area.  The rightmost Y marks the end of the pseudo-TM description.
And the scanhead of the UTM must initially point to the leftmost X which marks
one end of the machine condition area.

As this example shows, three sections of UTM tape must be specified, from left
to right.
Binary pseudo-tape:
   Pseudo-TM tape extends only to the left.  Contains only 0's and 1's and
   and an "M" that marks the location of the pseudo-TM read/write head.  (The
   M marker "hides" the symbol under it.)
Machine condition:
   This is a "working area" for the UTM: it lists the current state of the
   simulated TM, followed on the right by its currently scanned symbol (under
   the "M").  Generally, if there are N binary digits here, then the first N-1
   digits correspond to the pseudo-TM's current state and the last Nth digit
   corresponds to the symbol under the pseudo-TM's read/write head.
Quintuples:
   Each row of the pseudo-TM's state table is represented as a quintuple
   between X's (between X and a Y for the last state).   The order of listing
   the tuples does not matter, but each tuple must be described in the
   following format (suppose that there are N binary digits in the machine
   condition area):
      CST:  first N-1 digits - state number
      INP:  next digit       - input symbol
      NST:  next N-1 digits  - next state number
      OUT:  next digit       - output symbol
      MOV:  last digit       - direction of move (L=0, R=1)

In Minsky's notation, the state table of the above example pseudo-TM
description would be:

   CST INP | NST OUT MOV
   --------|-------------
    0   0  |  0   0   R
        1  |  1   0   R
    1   0  | halt 1   L
        1  |  1   1   R

Consider the third quintuple of the example which translates into the third row
of the above table.  "0101010" --> "01/0/10/1/0" specifies:

   CST=01  INP=0  |  NST=10    OUT=1  MOV=0,   in binary
   CST=1   INP=0  |  NST=2     OUT=1  MOV=0,   in decimal
   CST=1   INP=0  |  NST=halt  OUT=1  MOV=L,   in the row of the above table.

[Notice that state 2 does not exist in the example pseudo-TM, so the NST of
 this row equals a nonexistent state.  When Minsky's UTM searches for a next
 state accepting the input symbol under the scanhead (specified by the
 machine-condition) and it does not find it, then it will halt.  A nonexisting
 state for an NST ensures that the NST equals a halt state.]

In TM*'s notation the above table would be translated into:

   CST INP | OUT MOV NST
   ----------------------
    1   0  |  0   R   1
        1  |  0   R   2
    2   0  |  1   L   0
        1  |  1   R   2

The state numbers have to be changed here since in TM*, state 0 is always the
halt state.  The order of the column headings also differs from Minsky's table
notation.


                            How to run a UTM file
                            ---------------------
     To run a UTM input tape file (with a pseudo-TM on it), turn on the UTM
mode by selecting %!UTM mode from the MAIN-MENU if it is not already on.
Select %!Load from under the "Tape" column and type in the name of a stored
file from those listed.  Or, create a new UTM tape by selecting %!Enter (then
select %!Save from under the "Tape" column if you do not wish to lose it).
Now select %!Run and the UTM run screen will appear, including the UTM table
and the input tape from the file that you selected.
     The UTM display is unique.  The state table is arranged in four columns
with headers describing (tersely) what each column of code, or module, does:

(1) 'Locate'    - locates the current state of the pseudo-TM
(2) 'Copy'      - copies that state into the working-area
(3) 'Write dir' - determines which direction the head will move
(4) 'Simulate'  - performs the pseudo-tape operations

[ Suggestion:  Try loading and running UTMBUSY.INP ]

===============================================================================
                                 END OF HELP                                '99
